MODEL = [
 ("h2", "Service constraints and the integer configuration problem"),
 ("p", "A design is an integer number of pile-equipped berths k in a lot of fixed size. Berths without a device serve any vehicle; berths with a device serve a charging EV and, under the shared rule, may also hold a vehicle that only parks. The service standard is written on rates, each with an explicit numerator and denominator, and it is a vector rather than a scalar. The first rate is the share of admissible requests refused because no compatible berth is free at the moment of arrival. The second is the share of arriving EVs that fail to park, and the third is the same for ICEVs. A design is admissible at tolerance alpha when all three rates are valid and at or below alpha on every replication in a stated set, and the feasible set is then K = { k in the grid : the three rates are at or below alpha }. The shape of that set, not the identity of a single point, is what a planner can use: an interval, an empty set and a set with holes carry different information, and we report which one the enumeration produced, together with the smallest known admissible k where the grid ends before the constraint is met."),
 ("p", "Two categories are excluded from the count rather than scored. A rate whose denominator is zero because the scenario contains no vehicle of that class is not applicable and is recorded as such. A rate that cannot be evaluated because no vehicle of that class has left within the observation window is censored, and it is reported beside the constraint as an auxiliary quantity. Neither is treated as satisfied, and neither removes a candidate on its own. The energy shortfall rate is held to the second status throughout: it is reported with its censoring count and is not used as a success criterion in the main comparison, because the departure behaviour that would close the accounting is not observed in the panel. Where the grid is truncated we write that the grid does not settle the question; we do not write that a configuration is infeasible."),
 ("h2", "Information conditions and admissible decisions"),
 ("p", "The rule that decides who may use which berth is a function of the information available at the moment of decision, and we separate two conditions instead of mixing them. In condition I0 the rule sees what an operating system sees: the class of the arriving vehicle, the current occupancy and the history of events up to that instant. It does not see the charging intention of the arriving vehicle, its energy requirement, its true departure time or any future arrival. In condition I1 the rule sees one additional field, the charging intention of the vehicle arriving now, and nothing else changes: arrivals, dwell times and energy requests are identical, every vehicle still parks, and the information alters only the decision. Condition I1 is an analysis-only regime, flagged in every output, and it is not a proposal for how a lot should be operated, because reading an intention requires a channel that does not exist."),
 ("p", "The relation between the two conditions is one of containment: a rule available under I0 is available under I1, so the best value attainable under I1 is never worse. That containment is a property of the comparison and not a result, and we do not present it as one. What we report is the difference in service at a fixed k and in the feasible set when the additional field is present, with the caveat that the policies implemented for I1 are heuristics. A comparison between two heuristics is an algorithmic comparison and not an optimum, and where the two conditions differ we do not attribute the difference to information alone unless the policies are nested."),
]


